# Copy to .env for local development. Never commit any real .env file.
#
# Per-environment builds read .env.staging / .env.production instead, through
# ./scripts/build-web.sh — see docs/DNS.md.

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Worker that does AI tagging (see worker/).
#   local       http://localhost:8787      (npx wrangler dev, run from worker/)
#   staging     https://snaplocker-api-staging.<subdomain>.workers.dev
#   production  https://snaplocker-api.<subdomain>.workers.dev
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8787

# ── Optional: point at a different Firebase project ─────────────────────────
# Leave these out to use the project checked into src/lib/firebase-config.ts.
# Set them all to give an environment its own data — the only way test items stay
# out of the real catalog, since Firestore has no notion of a staging database.
#
# Doing this also needs, in the new project: Google sign-in enabled, the Firestore
# rules from README, and every origin added to Authorized domains.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# The Groq key is NOT here. It lives on the Worker as a secret:
#   cd worker && npx wrangler secret put GROQ_API_KEY --env <staging|production>
# Anything prefixed EXPO_PUBLIC_ is embedded in the built JavaScript in plain text.
